title: 'Test stance: end-to-end UX validation over piecemeal agent tests'
summary: 'Owner rejected piecemeal validation (separate @atelier-quill reconstruct + @atelier-iris compose tests via Claude Code chat) in favor of testing the full creator experience once the studio-UI bundle lands. The reasoning: chat-driven agent tests are still effectively manual command execution; the real signal comes from drag-drop in the browser + watching the canvas populate live + the agents working invisibly underneath. Aligns with TD-2026-05-27-723 (local-only-by-default, no-manual-friction) by treating the UI as the canonical test surface, not a polish on top of validated CLI flows. Implication: studio-UI bundle is the v1 validation bottleneck — when it lands cleanly and the end-to-end flow works (drop video → see captions + canvas populate → agents converse), v1 is ready to publish. No need to validate the underlying CLI pipeline separately; if the UI flow works, the foundation by definition works.'
symbols_touched:
  - '#cli-studio'
  - '#paradigm-augment'
  - '#cli-media'
  - '#quill-showrunner'
  - '#iris-composer'
learnings:
  - Test-stance decisions matter — validating piecemeal IS a creator-experience anti-pattern when the goal is to ship a self-contained creator tool. The piecemeal CLI flow we've been demoing is dev-tooling, not the product.
  - The studio-UI bundle is the load-bearing v1 gate. Everything before this (artifact schemas, agents, workspace, symbiosis, media pipeline) was foundation; the UI is the only thing the creator actually touches.
